\id NEH - Berean Standard Bible
\h Nehemiah
\toc2 Nehemiah
\toc1 Nehemiah
\mt1 Nehemiah
\c 1
\s1 Nehemiah’s Prayer
\r (Deuteronomy 30:1–10)
\m
\v 1 These are the words of \zwd Nehemiah son of \zwd Hacaliah|id="Q5637034" t="2"\zwd*|Q1025598\zwd*:
\b
\m \zdate|Q189689:P39:Q3114558:P580+P19Y>Kislev\*In the month of Chislev,\f + \fr 1:1 \ft Chislev is the ninth month of the Hebrew lunar calendar, usually occurring within the months of November and December.\f* in the twentieth year, while I was in the citadel of Susa,
\v 2 \zwd Hanani|Q137798603\zwd*, one of my brothers, arrived with men from Judah. So I questioned them about the remnant of the Jews who had survived the exile, and also about Jerusalem.
\b
\m
\v 3 And they told me, “The remnant who survived the exile are there in the province, in great trouble and disgrace. The wall of Jerusalem is broken down, and its gates have been burned with fire.”
\b
\m
\v 4 When I heard these words, I sat down and wept. I mourned for days, fasting and praying before the God of heaven.
\b
\m
\v 5 Then I said:
\b
\pmo “O LORD, God of heaven, the great and awesome God who keeps His covenant of loving devotion \f + \fr 1:5 \ft Forms of the Hebrew chesed are translated here and in most cases throughout the Scriptures as loving devotion; the range of meaning includes love, goodness, kindness, faithfulness, and mercy, as well as loyalty to a covenant.\f* with those who love Him and keep His commandments,
\v 6 let Your eyes be open and Your ears attentive to hear the prayer that I, Your servant, now pray before You day and night for Your servants, the Israelites.
\b
\pmo I confess the sins that we Israelites have committed against You. Both I and my father’s house have sinned.
\v 7 We have behaved corruptly against You and have not kept the commandments, statutes, and ordinances that You gave Your servant \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd*.
\b
\pmo
\v 8 Remember, I pray, the word that You commanded Your servant \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd* when You said, ‘If you are unfaithful, I will scatter you among the nations,
\v 9 but if you return to Me and keep and practice My commandments, then even if your exiles have been banished to the farthest horizon,\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Or to the extremity of the heavens\f* I will gather them from there and bring them to the place I have chosen as a dwelling for My Name.’\f + \fr 1:9 \ft Deuteronomy 30:1–4\f*
\b
\pmo
\v 10 They are Your servants and Your people. You redeemed them by Your great power and mighty hand.
\v 11 O Lord, may Your ear be attentive to my prayer and to the prayers of Your servants \f + \fr 1:11 \ft Literally to the prayer of Your servant and to the prayer of Your servants\f* who delight to revere Your name. Give Your servant success this day, I pray, and grant him mercy in the sight of this man.”
\b
\m (At that time I was the cupbearer to \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*.)
\c 2
\s1 Nehemiah Sent to Jerusalem
\m 
\v 1 Now in the month of Nisan,\f + \fr 2:1 \ft Nisan is the first month of the Hebrew lunar calendar, usually occurring within the months of March and April.\f* in the twentieth year of \zwd King Artaxerxes|Q189689\zwd*, when wine was set before him, I took the wine and gave it to \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*. I had never been sad in his presence, 
\v 2 so \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd* said to me, “Why is your face sad, though you are not ill? This could only be sadness of the heart.” 
\b
\m I was overwhelmed with fear 
\v 3 and replied to \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*, “May \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd* live forever! Why should I not be sad when the city where my fathers are buried lies in ruins, and its gates have been destroyed by fire?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 “What is your request?” replied \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*. 
\b
\m So I prayed to the God of heaven 
\v 5 and answered \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*, “If it pleases \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*, and if \zwd your servant|Q1025598\zwd* has found favor in your sight, I ask that you send me to Judah, to the city where my fathers are buried, so that I may rebuild it.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 Then \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*, with the queen seated beside him, asked me, “How long will your journey take, and when will you return?” So it pleased \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd* to send me, and I set a time. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 I also said to him, “If it pleases \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*, may letters be given to me for the governors west of the Euphrates,\f + \fr 2:7 \ft Hebrew beyond the River; also in verse 9\f* so that they will grant me safe passage until I reach Judah. 
\v 8 And may I have a letter to \zwd Asaph, keeper of \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*’s forest|Q131753159\zwd*, so that he will give me timber to make beams for the gates of the citadel to the temple, for the city wall, and for the house I will occupy.” 
\b
\m And because the gracious hand of my God was upon me, \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd* granted my requests. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Then I went to the \zwd governors west of the Euphrates|\zwd* and gave them \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*’s letters. \zwd The king|Q189689\zwd* had also sent army officers and cavalry with me. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 But when \zwd Sanballat the Horonite|Q751918\zwd* and \zwd Tobiah the Ammonite official|Q4201578\zwd* heard about this, they were deeply disturbed that someone had come to seek the well-being of the Israelites. 
\s1 Nehemiah Inspects the Walls
\b
\m 
\v 11 After I had arrived in Jerusalem and had been there three days, 
\v 12 I set out at night with a few men. I did not tell anyone what my God had laid on my heart to do for Jerusalem. The only animal with me was the one on which I was riding. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So I went out at night through the Valley Gate toward the Well of the Serpent \f + \fr 2:13 \ft Or Dragon or Jackal\f* and the Dung Gate, and I inspected the walls of Jerusalem that had been broken down and the gates that had been destroyed by fire. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 Then I went on to the Fountain Gate and the King’s Pool, but there was no room for the animal under me to get through; 
\v 15 so I went up the valley by night and inspected the wall. Then I headed back and reentered through the Valley Gate. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 The officials did not know where I had gone or what I was doing, for I had not yet told the Jews or priests or nobles or officials or any other workers. 
\v 17 Then I said to them, “You see the trouble we are in. Jerusalem lies in ruins, and its gates have been burned down. Come, let us rebuild the wall of Jerusalem, so that we will no longer be a disgrace.” 
\b
\m 
\v 18 I also told them about the gracious hand of my God upon me, and what \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd* had said to me. 
\b
\m “Let us start rebuilding,” they replied, and they set their hands to this good work. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 But when \zwd Sanballat the Horonite|Q751918\zwd*, \zwd Tobiah the Ammonite official|Q4201578\zwd*, and \zwd Geshem the Arab|Q4290819\zwd* heard about this, they mocked us and ridiculed us, saying, “What is this you are doing? Are you rebelling against \zwd the king|Q189689\zwd*?” 
\b
\m 
\v 20 So I answered them and said, “The God of heaven is the One who will grant us success. We, His servants, will start rebuilding, but you have no portion, right, or claim in Jerusalem.” 
\c 3
\s1 The Builders of the Walls
\m 
\v 1 At the Sheep Gate, \zwd Eliashib the high priest|Q9252723\zwd* and his fellow priests began rebuilding. They dedicated it and installed its doors. After building as far as the Tower of the Hundred and the Tower of \zwd Hananel|id="Q135646994" t="2"\zwd*, they dedicated the wall. 
\v 2 The men of Jericho built next to \zwd Eliashib|Q9252723\zwd*, and \zwd Zaccur son of \zwd Imri|\zwd*|Q137798632\zwd* built next to them. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 The Fish Gate was rebuilt by the sons of Hassenaah. They laid its beams and installed its doors, bolts, and bars. 
\v 4 Next to them, \zwd Meremoth son of \zwd Uriah|\zwd*|\zwd*, the son of \zwd Hakkoz|\zwd*, made repairs. Next to him, \zwd Meshullam son of \zwd Berechiah|\zwd*|\zwd*, the son of \zwd Meshezabel|\zwd*, made repairs; and next to him, \zwd Zadok son of \zwd Baana|id="Q139591988" t="2"\zwd*|Q138862854\zwd* made repairs as well. 
\v 5 Next to him, the Tekoites made repairs, but their nobles did not put their shoulders to the work under their supervisors.\f + \fr 3:5 \ft Or their Lord or the governor\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 6 The Jeshanah Gate \f + \fr 3:6 \ft Or The Old City Gate\f* was repaired by \zwd Joiada son of \zwd Paseah|\zwd*|\zwd* and \zwd Meshullam son of \zwd Besodeiah|\zwd*|\zwd*. They laid its beams and installed its doors, bolts, and bars. 
\v 7 Next to them, repairs were made by \zwd Melatiah the Gibeonite|\zwd*, \zwd Jadon the Meronothite|\zwd*, and the men of Gibeon and Mizpah, who were under the authority of the governor of the region west of the Euphrates.\f + \fr 3:7 \ft Hebrew the governor beyond the River\f* 
\v 8 Next to them, \zwd Uzziel son of \zwd Harhaiah|\zwd*|\zwd*, one of the goldsmiths, made repairs. And next to him, \zwd Hananiah|\zwd*, one of the perfumers, made repairs. They fortified Jerusalem as far as the Broad Wall. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 Next to them, \zwd Rephaiah son of \zwd Hur|\zwd*|\zwd*, ruler of a half-district of Jerusalem, made repairs; 
\v 10 next to him, \zwd Jedaiah son of \zwd Harumaph|\zwd*|\zwd* made repairs across from his house; and next to him, \zwd Hattush son of \zwd Hashabneiah|\zwd*|\zwd* made repairs. 
\v 11 \zwd Malchijah son of \zwd Harim|\zwd*|\zwd* and \zwd Hasshub son of \zwd Pahath-moab|\zwd*|\zwd* repaired another section, as well as the Tower of the Ovens. 
\v 12 And next to them, \zwd Shallum son of \zwd Hallohesh|\zwd*|\zwd*, ruler of the other half-district of Jerusalem, made repairs, with the help of \zwd his daughters|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 13 The Valley Gate was repaired by \zwd Hanun|\zwd* and the residents of Zanoah. They rebuilt it, installed its doors, bolts, and bars, and repaired a thousand cubits \f + \fr 3:13 \ft 1,000 cubits is approximately 1,500 feet or 457.2 meters.\f* of the wall as far as the Dung Gate. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 The Dung Gate was repaired by \zwd Malchijah son of \zwd Rechab|\zwd*|\zwd*, ruler of the district of Beth-haccherem. He rebuilt it and installed its doors, bolts, and bars. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 The Fountain Gate was repaired by \zwd Shallun \f + \fr 3:15 \ft Hebrew; Syriac Shallum\f* son of \zwd Col-hozeh|\zwd*|\zwd*, ruler of the district of Mizpah. He rebuilt it, roofed it, and installed its doors, bolts, and bars. He also repaired the wall of the Pool of Shelah \f + \fr 3:15 \ft Pool of Shelah is another name for the Pool of Siloam.\f* near the king’s garden, as far as the stairs that descend from the City of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 16 Beyond him, \zwd Nehemiah son of \zwd Azbuk|id="Q139574817" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*, ruler of a half-district of Beth-zur, made repairs up to a point opposite the tombs of \zwd David|id="Q41370" t="2"\zwd*, as far as the artificial pool and the House of the Mighty. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Next to him, the Levites made repairs under \zwd Rehum son of \zwd Bani|\zwd*|\zwd*, and next to him, \zwd Hashabiah|\zwd*, ruler of a half-district of Keilah, made repairs for his district. 
\v 18 Next to him, their countrymen made repairs under \zwd Binnui \f + \fr 3:18 \ft Two Hebrew manuscripts and Syriac (see also LXX and Nehemiah 3:24); most Hebrew manuscripts Bavvai\f* son of \zwd Henadad|\zwd*|Q140133383\zwd*, ruler of the other half-district of Keilah. 
\v 19 And next to him, \zwd Ezer son of \zwd Jeshua|\zwd*|\zwd*, ruler of Mizpah, repaired another section opposite the ascent to the armory, near the angle in the wall. 
\b
\m 
\v 20 Next to him, \zwd Baruch son of \zwd Zabbai|id="Q138862662" t="2"\zwd*|Q139969885\zwd* diligently repaired another section, from the angle to the doorway of the house of \zwd Eliashib the high priest|id="Q9252723" t="2"\zwd*. 
\v 21 Next to him, \zwd Meremoth son of \zwd Uriah|\zwd*|\zwd*, the son of \zwd Hakkoz|\zwd*, repaired another section, from the doorway of the house of \zwd Eliashib|id="Q9252723" t="2"\zwd* to the end of the house. 
\v 22 And next to him, the priests from the surrounding area made repairs. 
\b
\m 
\v 23 Beyond them, \zwd Benjamin|Q140316308\zwd* and \zwd Hasshub|\zwd* made repairs in front of their house, and next to them, \zwd Azariah son of \zwd Maaseiah|\zwd*|Q139567993\zwd*, the son of \zwd Ananiah|id="Q138727580" t="2"\zwd*, made repairs beside his house. 
\v 24 After him, \zwd Binnui son of \zwd Henadad|\zwd*|\zwd* repaired another section, from the house of \zwd Azariah|Q139567993\zwd* to the angle and the corner, 
\v 25 and \zwd Palal son of \zwd Uzai|\zwd*|\zwd* made repairs opposite the angle and the tower that juts out from the upper palace of the king near the courtyard of the guard. Next to him, \zwd Pedaiah son of \zwd Parosh|\zwd*|\zwd* 
\v 26 and the temple servants \f + \fr 3:26 \ft Hebrew the Nethinim; also in verse 31\f* living on the hill of Ophel made repairs opposite the Water Gate toward the east and the tower that juts out. 
\v 27 And next to them, the Tekoites repaired another section, from a point opposite the great tower that juts out to the wall of Ophel. 
\b
\m 
\v 28 Above the Horse Gate, each of the priests made repairs in front of his own house. 
\v 29 Next to them, \zwd Zadok son of \zwd Immer|\zwd*|Q138862863\zwd* made repairs opposite his house, and next to him, \zwd Shemaiah son of \zwd Shecaniah|\zwd*|\zwd*, the guard of the East Gate, made repairs. 
\b
\m 
\v 30 Next to him, \zwd Hananiah son of \zwd Shelemiah|id="Q140131632" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*, as well as \zwd Hanun the sixth son of \zwd Zalaph|id="Q139008748" t="2"\zwd*|\zwd*, repaired another section. Next to them, \zwd Meshullam son of \zwd Berechiah|\zwd*|\zwd* made repairs opposite his own quarters. 
\v 31 Next to him, \zwd Malchijah|\zwd*, one of the goldsmiths, made repairs as far as the house of the temple servants and the merchants, opposite the Inspection Gate, and as far as the upper room above the corner. 
\v 32 And between the upper room above the corner and the Sheep Gate, the goldsmiths and merchants made repairs. 
\c 4
\s1 The Work Ridiculed
\m 
\v 1 Now when \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd* heard that we were rebuilding the wall, he was furious and filled with indignation. He ridiculed the Jews 
\v 2 before his associates and the army of Samaria, saying, “What are these feeble Jews doing? Can they restore the wall by themselves?\f + \fr 4:2 \ft Or Will they commit themselves to God?\f* Will they offer sacrifices? Will they complete it in a day? Can they bring these burnt stones back to life from the mounds of rubble?” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Then \zwd Tobiah the Ammonite|Q4201578\zwd*, who was beside him, said, “If even a fox were to climb up on what they are building, it would break down their wall of stones!” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Hear us, O God, for we are despised. Turn their scorn back upon their own heads, and let them be taken as plunder to a land of captivity. 
\v 5 Do not cover up their iniquity or let their sin be blotted out from Your sight, for they have provoked the builders.\f + \fr 4:5 \ft Or have provoked You to anger before the builders\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 6 So we rebuilt the wall until all of it was joined together up to half its height, for the people had a mind to work. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 When \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd* and \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd*, together with the Arabs, Ammonites, and Ashdodites, heard that the repair to the walls of Jerusalem was progressing and that the gaps were being closed, they were furious, 
\v 8 and all of them conspired to come and fight against Jerusalem and create a hindrance. 
\s1 Discouragement Overcome
\b
\m 
\v 9 So we prayed to our God and posted a guard against them day and night. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Meanwhile, the people of Judah said:\f + \fr 4:10 \ft Or Meanwhile, Judah said\f* 
\b
\q1 “The strength of the laborer fails, 
\q2 and there is so much rubble 
\q1 that we will never be able 
\q2 to rebuild the wall.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And our enemies said, “Before they know or see a thing, we will come into their midst, kill them, and put an end to the work.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 At that time the Jews who lived nearby came and told us ten times over, “Wherever you turn, they will attack us.” 
\b
\m 
\v 13 So I stationed men behind the lowest sections of the wall, at the vulnerable areas. I stationed them by families with their swords, spears, and bows. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 After I had made an inspection, I stood up and said to the nobles, the officials, and the rest of the people, “Do not be afraid of them. Remember the Lord, who is great and awesome, and fight for your brothers, your sons and your daughters, your wives and your homes.” 
\b
\m 
\v 15 When our enemies heard that we were aware of their scheme and that God had frustrated it, each of us returned to his own work on the wall. 
\v 16 And from that day on, half of my servants did the work while the other half held spears, shields, bows, and armor. 
\b
\m The officers stationed themselves behind all the people of Judah 
\v 17 who were rebuilding the wall. The laborers who carried materials worked with one hand and held a weapon with the other. 
\v 18 And each of the builders worked with his sword strapped at his side. But the trumpeter stayed beside me. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Then I said to the nobles, the officials, and the rest of the people: “The work is great and extensive, and we are spread out far from one another along the wall. 
\v 20 Wherever you hear the sound of the horn, rally to us there. Our God will fight for us!” 
\b
\m 
\v 21 So we continued the work, while half of the men held spears from the break of dawn until the stars came out. 
\v 22 At that time I also said to the people, “Let every man and his servant spend the night inside Jerusalem, so that they can stand guard by night and work by day.” 
\b
\m 
\v 23 So neither I nor my brothers nor my servants nor the guards with me changed out of our clothes; each carried his weapon, even to go for water.\f + \fr 4:23 \ft Or each carried his weapon in his right hand\f* 
\c 5
\s1 Nehemiah Defends the Oppressed
\m 
\v 1 About that time there was a great outcry from the people and their wives against their fellow Jews. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Some were saying, “We and our sons and daughters are numerous. We must get grain in order to eat and stay alive.” 
\b
\m 
\v 3 Others were saying, “We are mortgaging our fields, our vineyards, and our homes to get grain during the famine.” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Still others were saying, “We have borrowed money to pay the king’s tax on our fields and vineyards. 
\v 5 We and our children are just like our countrymen and their children, yet we are subjecting our sons and daughters to slavery. Some of our daughters are already enslaved, but we are powerless to redeem them because our fields and vineyards belong to others.” 
\b
\m 
\v 6 When I heard their outcry and these complaints, I became extremely angry, 
\v 7 and after serious thought I rebuked the nobles and officials, saying, “You are exacting usury from your own brothers!” 
\b
\m So I called a large assembly against them 
\v 8 and said, “We have done our best to buy back our Jewish brothers who were sold to foreigners, but now you are selling your own brothers, that they may be sold back to us!” 
\b
\m But they remained silent, for they could find nothing to say. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 So I continued, “What you are doing is not right. Shouldn’t you walk in the fear of our God to avoid the reproach of our foreign enemies? 
\v 10 I, as well as my brothers and my servants, have been lending the people money and grain. Please, let us stop this usury. 
\v 11 Please restore to them immediately their fields, vineyards, olive groves, and houses, along with the percentage of the money, grain, new wine, and oil that you have been assessing them.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 “We will restore it,” they replied, “and will require nothing more from them. We will do as you say.” 
\b
\m So I summoned the priests and required of the nobles and officials an oath that they would do what they had promised. 
\v 13 I also shook out the folds of my robe and said, “May God likewise shake out of his house and possessions every man who does not keep this promise. May such a man be shaken out and have nothing!” 
\b
\m The whole assembly said, “Amen,” and they praised the LORD. And the people did as they had promised. 
\s1 Nehemiah’s Generosity
\b
\m 
\v 14 Furthermore, from the day \zwd King Artaxerxes|Q189689\zwd* appointed me to be their governor in the land of Judah, from his twentieth year until his thirty-second year (twelve years total), neither I nor my brothers ate the food allotted to the governor. 
\b
\m 
\v 15 The governors before me had heavily burdened the people, taking from them bread and wine plus forty shekels of silver.\f + \fr 5:15 \ft 40 shekels is approximately 1 pound or 453.6 grams of silver.\f* Their servants also oppressed the people. But I did not do this, because of my fear of God. 
\v 16 Instead, I devoted myself to the construction of the wall, and all my servants were gathered there for the work; we did not acquire any land. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 There were 150 Jews and officials at my table, besides the guests from the surrounding nations. 
\v 18 Each day one ox, six choice sheep, and some fowl were prepared for me, and once every ten days an abundance of all kinds of wine was provided. But I did not demand the food allotted to the governor, because the burden on the people was so heavy. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Remember me favorably, O my God, for all that I have done for this people. 
\c 6
\s1 Sanballat’s Conspiracy
\m 
\v 1 When \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd*, \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd*, \zwd Geshem the Arab|Q4290819\zwd*, and the rest of our enemies heard that I had rebuilt the wall and not a gap was left—though to that time I had not yet installed the doors in the gates—
\v 2 \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd* and \zwd Geshem|Q4290819\zwd* sent me this message: “Come, let us meet together in one of the villages \f + \fr 6:2 \ft Or in Kephirim\f* on the plain \f + \fr 6:2 \ft Or in the valley\f* of Ono.” 
\b
\m But they were planning to harm me. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 So I sent messengers to them, saying, “I am doing a great work and cannot come down. Why should the work stop while I leave it to go down to you?” 
\b
\m 
\v 4 Four times they sent me the same message, and each time I gave the same reply. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 The fifth time, \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd* sent me this same message by his young servant, who had in his hand an unsealed letter 
\v 6 that read: 
\b
\pmo “It is reported among the nations—and Geshem \f + \fr 6:6 \ft Hebrew Gashmu, a variant of Geshem; see verse 1.\f* agrees—that you and the Jews are plotting to revolt, and this is why you are building the wall. According to these reports, you are to become their king, 
\v 7 and you have even appointed prophets in Jerusalem to proclaim on your behalf: ‘There is a king in Judah.’ Soon these rumors will reach the ears of the king. So come, let us confer together.” 
\b
\m 
\v 8 Then I sent him this reply: “There is nothing to these rumors you are spreading; you are inventing them in your own mind.” 
\b
\m 
\v 9 For they were all trying to frighten us, saying, “Their hands will be weakened in the work, and it will never be finished.” 
\b
\m But now, my God,\f + \fr 6:9 \ft Hebrew But now; for clarity, my God has been included.\f* strengthen my hands. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Later, I went to the house of \zwd Shemaiah son of \zwd Delaiah|\zwd*|Q137798637\zwd*, the son of \zwd Mehetabel|\zwd*, who was confined to his house. He said: 
\b
\q1 “Let us meet at the house of God 
\q2 inside the temple. 
\q1 Let us shut the temple doors 
\q2 because they are coming to kill you—
\q2 by night they are coming to kill you!” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 But I replied, “Should a man like me run away? Should one like me go into the temple to save his own life? I will not go!” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 I realized that God had not sent him, but that he had uttered this prophecy against me because \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd* and \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd* had hired him. 
\v 13 He had been hired to intimidate me so that I would sin by doing as he suggested, so they could give me a bad name in order to discredit me. 
\b
\m 
\v 14 O my God, remember \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd* and \zwd Sanballat|Q751918\zwd* for what they have done, and also \zwd Noadiah the prophetess|Q112991274\zwd* and the other prophets who tried to intimidate me. 
\s1 Completion of the Wall
\b
\m 
\v 15 So the wall was completed in fifty-two days, on the twenty-fifth of Elul.\f + \fr 6:15 \ft Elul is the sixth month of the Hebrew lunar calendar, usually occurring within the months of August and September.\f* 
\v 16 When all our enemies heard about this, all the surrounding nations were afraid and disheartened, for they realized that this task had been accomplished by our God. 
\b
\m 
\v 17 Also in those days, the nobles of Judah sent many letters to \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd*, and \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd*’s letters kept coming to them. 
\v 18 For many in Judah were bound by oath to him, since he was a son-in-law of \zwd Shecaniah son of \zwd Arah|id="Q138757378" t="2"\zwd*|Q140160096\zwd*, and his son \zwd Jehohanan|\zwd* had married \zwd the daughter of \zwd Meshullam son of \zwd Berechiah|\zwd*|\zwd*|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 19 Moreover, these nobles kept reporting to me \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd*’s good deeds, and they relayed my words to him. And \zwd Tobiah|Q4201578\zwd* sent letters to intimidate me. 
\c 7
\s1 Securing the City
\m 
\v 1 When the wall had been rebuilt and I had set the doors in place, the gatekeepers, singers, and Levites were appointed. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 Then I put \zwd my brother Hanani|Q137798603\zwd* in charge of Jerusalem, along with \zwd Hananiah the commander of the fortress|\zwd*, because he was a faithful man who feared God more than most. 
\v 3 And I told them, “Do not open the gates of Jerusalem until the sun is hot. While the guards are on duty, keep the doors shut and securely fastened. And appoint the residents of Jerusalem as guards, some at their posts and some at their own homes.” 
\s1 The List of Returning Exiles
\r (Ezra 2:1–67)
\b
\m 
\v 4 Now the city was large and spacious, but there were few people in it, and the houses had not yet been rebuilt. 
\v 5 Then my God put it into my heart to assemble the nobles, the officials, and the people to be enrolled by genealogy. I found the genealogical register of those who had first returned, and I found the following written in it: 
\b
\m 
\v 6 These are the people of the province who came up from the captivity of the exiles carried away to Babylon by Nebuchadnezzar its king. They returned to Jerusalem and Judah, each to his own town, 
\v 7 accompanied by \zwd Zerubbabel|Q320139\zwd*, \zwd Jeshua|\zwd*, \zwd Nehemiah|\zwd*, \zwd Azariah|\zwd*, \zwd Raamiah|\zwd*, \zwd Nahamani|\zwd*, \zwd Mordecai|\zwd*, \zwd Bilshan|\zwd*, \zwd Mispereth|Q6884731\zwd*, \zwd Bigvai|\zwd*, \zwd Nehum|\zwd*, and \zwd Baanah|\zwd*.\f + \fr 7:7 \ft Parallel text at Ezra 2:2 accompanied by Zerubbabel, Jeshua, Nehemiah, Seraiah, Reelaiah, Mordecai, Bilshan, Mispar, Bigvai, Rehum, and Baanah\f* 
\b
\m This is the count of the men of Israel: 
\b
\li1 
\v 8 the descendants of \zwd Parosh|\zwd*, 2,172; 
\b
\li1 
\v 9 the descendants of \zwd Shephatiah|\zwd*, 372; 
\b
\li1 
\v 10 the descendants of \zwd Arah|id="Q138782353" t="2"\zwd*, 652; 
\b
\li1 
\v 11 the descendants of \zwd Pahath-moab|\zwd* (through the line of \zwd Jeshua|\zwd* and \zwd Joab|\zwd*), 2,818; 
\b
\li1 
\v 12 the descendants of \zwd Elam|\zwd*, 1,254; 
\b
\li1 
\v 13 the descendants of \zwd Zattu|id="Q138862840" t="2"\zwd*, 845; 
\b
\li1 
\v 14 the descendants of \zwd Zaccai|id="Q139008708" t="2"\zwd*, 760; 
\b
\li1 
\v 15 the descendants of \zwd Binnui|\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:15 \ft Binnui is a variant of Bani; see Ezra 2:10.\f* 648; 
\b
\li1 
\v 16 the descendants of \zwd Bebai|id="Q65303005" t="2"\zwd*, 628; 
\b
\li1 
\v 17 the descendants of \zwd Azgad|id="Q139575116" t="2"\zwd*, 2,322; 
\b
\li1 
\v 18 the descendants of \zwd Adonikam|\zwd*, 667; 
\b
\li1 
\v 19 the descendants of \zwd Bigvai|\zwd*, 2,067; 
\b
\li1 
\v 20 the descendants of \zwd Adin|\zwd*, 655; 
\b
\li1 
\v 21 the descendants of \zwd Ater|id="Q139075889" t="2"\zwd* (through \zwd Hezekiah|\zwd*), 98; 
\b
\li1 
\v 22 the descendants of \zwd Hashum|\zwd*, 328; 
\b
\li1 
\v 23 the descendants of \zwd Bezai|\zwd*, 324; 
\b
\li1 
\v 24 the descendants of \zwd Hariph|\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:24 \ft Hariph is a variant of Jorah; see Ezra 2:18.\f* 112; 
\b
\li1 
\v 25 the descendants of \zwd Gibeon|\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:25 \ft Gibeon is a variant of Gibbar; see Ezra 2:20.\f* 95; 
\b
\li1 
\v 26 the men of Bethlehem and Netophah, 188; 
\b
\li1 
\v 27 the men of Anathoth, 128; 
\b
\li1 
\v 28 the men of Beth-azmaveth,\f + \fr 7:28 \ft Beth-azmaveth is a variant of Azmaveth; see Ezra 2:24.\f* 42; 
\b
\li1 
\v 29 the men of Kiriath-jearim, Chephirah, and Beeroth, 743; 
\b
\li1 
\v 30 the men of Ramah and Geba, 621; 
\b
\li1 
\v 31 the men of Michmash, 122; 
\b
\li1 
\v 32 the men of Bethel and Ai, 123; 
\b
\li1 
\v 33 the men of the other Nebo,\f + \fr 7:33 \ft Or of West Nebo\f* 52; 
\b
\li1 
\v 34 the descendants of the other Elam,\f + \fr 7:34 \ft Or of West Elam\f* 1,254; 
\b
\li1 
\v 35 the descendants of Harim, 320; 
\b
\li1 
\v 36 the men \f + \fr 7:36 \ft Literally the sons; here and in verse 37\f* of Jericho, 345; 
\b
\li1 
\v 37 the men of Lod, Hadid, and Ono, 721; 
\b
\li1 
\v 38 and the descendants of Senaah, 3,930. 
\b
\m 
\v 39 The priests: 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Jedaiah|\zwd* (through the house of \zwd Jeshua|\zwd*), 973; 
\b
\li1 
\v 40 the descendants of \zwd Immer|\zwd*, 1,052; 
\b
\li1 
\v 41 the descendants of \zwd Pashhur|\zwd*, 1,247; 
\b
\li1 
\v 42 and the descendants of \zwd Harim|\zwd*, 1,017. 
\b
\m 
\v 43 The Levites: 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Jeshua|\zwd* (through \zwd Kadmiel|\zwd*, through the line of \zwd Hodevah|\zwd* \f + \fr 7:43 \ft Hodevah is a variant of Hodaviah; see Ezra 2:40.\f*), 74. 
\b
\m 
\v 44 The singers: 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Asaph|\zwd*, 148. 
\b
\m 
\v 45 The gatekeepers: 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Shallum|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Ater|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Talmon|id="Q139678428" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Akkub|id="Q138713763" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Hatita|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and the descendants of \zwd Shobai|id="Q139890037" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li2 138 in all. 
\b
\m 
\v 46 The temple servants:\f + \fr 7:46 \ft Hebrew The Nethinim; also in verses 60 and 73\f* 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Ziha|id="Q138834645" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Hasupha|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Tabbaoth|id="Q139691790" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 47 the descendants of \zwd Keros|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Sia|\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:47 \ft Sia is a variant of Siaha; see Ezra 2:44.\f* 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Padon|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 48 the descendants of \zwd Lebanah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Hagabah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Shalmai|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 49 the descendants of \zwd Hanan|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Giddel|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Gahar|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 50 the descendants of \zwd Reaiah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Rezin|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Nekoda|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 51 the descendants of \zwd Gazzam|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Uzza|id="Q138713964" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Paseah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 52 the descendants of \zwd Besai|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Meunim|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Nephushesim|\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:52 \ft Nephushesim is a variant of Nephusim; see Ezra 2:50.\f* 
\b
\li1 
\v 53 the descendants of \zwd Bakbuk|id="Q139601184" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Hakupha|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Harhur|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 54 the descendants of \zwd Bazlith|id="Q140133508" t="2"\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:54 \ft Bazlith is a variant of Bazluth; see Ezra 2:52.\f* 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Mehida|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Harsha|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 55 the descendants of \zwd Barkos|id="Q139969310" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Sisera|id="Q139780600" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Temah|id="Q139592150" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 56 the descendants of \zwd Neziah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and the descendants of \zwd Hatipha|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 57 The descendants of the servants of \zwd Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd*: 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Sotai|id="Q139741904" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Sophereth|id="Q139741971" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Perida|\zwd*,\f + \fr 7:57 \ft Perida is a variant of Peruda; see Ezra 2:55.\f* 
\b
\li1 
\v 58 the descendants of \zwd Jaala|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Darkon|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Giddel|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 59 the descendants of \zwd Shephatiah|id="Q140030834" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Hattil|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Pochereth-hazzebaim|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and the descendants of \zwd Amon|id="Q138721677" t="2"\zwd*.\f + \fr 7:59 \ft Amon is a variant of Ami; see Ezra 2:57.\f* 
\b
\li1 
\v 60 The temple servants and descendants of the servants of \zwd Solomon|id="Q37085" t="2"\zwd* numbered 392 in all. 
\b
\m 
\v 61 The following came up from Tel-melah, Tel-harsha, Cherub, Addon,\f + \fr 7:61 \ft Addon is a variant of Addan; see Ezra 2:59.\f* and Immer, but they could not prove that their families were descended from Israel: 
\b
\li1 
\v 62 the descendants of \zwd Delaiah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Tobiah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and the descendants of \zwd Nekoda|\zwd*, 
\b
\li2 642 in all. 
\b
\li1 
\v 63 And from among the priests: the descendants of \zwd Hobaiah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 the descendants of \zwd Hakkoz|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and the descendants of \zwd Barzillai|id="Q140047323" t="2"\zwd* (who had married a daughter of \zwd Barzillai the Gileadite|id="Q6710215" t="2"\zwd* and was called by their name). 
\b
\li1 
\v 64 These men searched for their family records, but they could not find them and so were excluded from the priesthood as unclean. 
\v 65 \zwd The governor|\zwd* ordered them not to eat the most holy things until there was a priest to consult the Urim and Thummim.\f + \fr 7:65 \ft Literally Lights and Perfections\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 66 The whole assembly numbered 42,360, 
\v 67 in addition to their 7,337 menservants and maidservants, as well as their 245 male and female singers. 
\v 68 They had 736 horses, 245 mules, \f + \fr 7:68 \ft Some Hebrew manuscripts (see also Ezra 2:66); most Hebrew manuscripts do not include this verse.\f* 
\v 69 435 camels, and 6,720 donkeys. 
\s1 Offerings by the Exiles
\r (Exodus 38:21–31; Ezra 2:68–70)
\b
\m 
\v 70 Some of the heads of the families contributed to the project. \zwd The governor|\zwd* gave to the treasury 1,000 darics of gold,\f + \fr 7:70 \ft Or 1,000 gold drachmas; that is, approximately 18.5 pounds or 8.4 kilograms of gold coins\f* 50 bowls, and 530 priestly garments. 
\v 71 And some of the heads of the families gave to the treasury for the project 20,000 darics of gold \f + \fr 7:71 \ft Or 20,000 gold drachmas; that is, approximately 370.4 pounds or 168 kilograms of gold coins; also in verse 72 \f* and 2,200 minas of silver.\f + \fr 7:71 \ft Or 2,200 silver minas; that is, approximately 1.38 tons or 1.25 metric tons of silver\f* 
\v 72 The rest of the people gave a total of 20,000 darics of gold, 2,000 minas of silver,\f + \fr 7:72 \ft Or 2,000 silver minas; that is, approximately 1.26 tons or 1.14 metric tons of silver\f* and 67 priestly garments. 
\b
\m 
\v 73 So the priests, Levites, gatekeepers, singers, and temple servants, along with some of the people and the rest of the Israelites, settled in their own towns. And by the seventh month the Israelites had settled in their towns. 
\c 8
\s1 Ezra Reads the Law
\r (Deuteronomy 31:9–13)
\m 
\v 1 At that time all the people gathered together in the square before the Water Gate, and they asked \zwd Ezra the scribe|Q191787\zwd* to bring out the Book of the Law of \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd*, which the LORD had commanded for Israel. 
\b
\m 
\v 2 On the first day of the seventh month, \zwd Ezra the priest|Q191787\zwd* brought the Law before the assembly of men and women and all who could listen and understand. 
\v 3 So \zwd Ezra|Q191787\zwd* read it aloud from daybreak until noon as he faced the square before the Water Gate, in front of the men and women and those who could understand. 
\b
\m And all the people listened attentively to the Book of the Law. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 \zwd Ezra the scribe|Q191787\zwd* stood on a high wooden platform built for this occasion. At his right side stood \zwd Mattithiah|\zwd*, \zwd Shema|Q140068852\zwd*, \zwd Anaiah|Q138716465\zwd*, \zwd Uriah|Q139104438\zwd*, \zwd Hilkiah|\zwd*, and \zwd Maaseiah|\zwd*, and at his left were \zwd Pedaiah|\zwd*, \zwd Mishael|\zwd*, \zwd Malchijah|\zwd*, \zwd Hashum|\zwd*, \zwd Hash-baddanah|\zwd*, \zwd Zechariah|Q138772237\zwd*, and \zwd Meshullam|\zwd*. 
\b
\m 
\v 5 \zwd Ezra|Q191787\zwd* opened the book in full view of all the people, since he was standing above them all, and as he opened it, all the people stood up. 
\v 6 Then \zwd Ezra|Q191787\zwd* blessed the LORD, the great God, and with their hands uplifted, all the people said, “Amen, Amen!” Then they bowed down and worshiped the LORD with their faces to the ground. 
\b
\m 
\v 7 The Levites—\zwd Jeshua|\zwd*, \zwd Bani|\zwd*, \zwd Sherebiah|\zwd*, \zwd Jamin|\zwd*, \zwd Akkub|Q138713794\zwd*, \zwd Shabbethai|Q140320787\zwd*, \zwd Hodiah|\zwd*, \zwd Maaseiah|\zwd*, \zwd Kelita|\zwd*, \zwd Azariah|Q139568083\zwd*, \zwd Jozabad|\zwd*, \zwd Hanan|\zwd*, and \zwd Pelaiah|\zwd*—instructed the people in the Law as they stood in their places. 
\v 8 So they read from the Book of the Law of God, explaining it \f + \fr 8:8 \ft Or translating it or paragraph by paragraph\f* and giving insight, so that the people could understand what was being read. 
\b
\m 
\v 9 \zwd Nehemiah the governor|Q1025598\zwd*, \zwd Ezra the priest and scribe|Q191787\zwd*, and the Levites who were instructing the people said to all of them, “This day is holy to the LORD your God. Do not mourn or weep.” 
\b
\m For all the people were weeping as they heard the words of the Law. 
\b
\m 
\v 10 Then \zwd Nehemiah|Q1025598\zwd* told them, “Go and eat what is rich, drink what is sweet, and send out portions to those who have nothing prepared, since today is holy to our Lord. Do not grieve, for the joy of the LORD is your strength.” 
\b
\m 
\v 11 And the Levites calmed all the people, saying, “Be still, since today is holy. Do not grieve.” 
\b
\m 
\v 12 Then all the people began to eat and drink, to send out portions, and to rejoice greatly, because they understood the words that had been made known to them. 
\s1 The Feast of Tabernacles
\r (Leviticus 23:33–44; Zechariah 14:16–21)
\b
\m 
\v 13 On the second day of the month, the heads of all the families, along with the priests and Levites, gathered around \zwd Ezra the scribe|Q191787\zwd* to study the words of the Law. 
\v 14 And they found written in the Law, which the LORD had commanded through \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd*, that the Israelites were to dwell in booths \f + \fr 8:14 \ft Or tabernacles or shelters; also in verses 15, 16, and 17\f* during the feast of the seventh month, 
\b
\m 
\v 15 and that they should proclaim this message and spread it throughout their towns and in Jerusalem, saying, “Go out to the hill country and bring back branches of olive, wild olive,\f + \fr 8:15 \ft Or pine; literally oil tree\f* myrtle, palm, and other leafy trees, to make booths, as it is written.”\f + \fr 8:15 \ft See Leviticus 23:37–40.\f* 
\b
\m 
\v 16 And the people went out, brought back branches, and made booths on their own rooftops, in their courtyards, in the courts of the house of God, and in the squares by the Water Gate and by the Gate of Ephraim. 
\v 17 The whole assembly that had returned from exile made booths and lived in them. From the days of \zwd Joshua \f + \fr 8:17 \ft Hebrew Jeshua, a variant of Joshua\f* son of \zwd Nun|id="Q3344859" t="2"\zwd*|id="Q7734" t="2"\zwd* until that day, the Israelites had not celebrated like this. And there was great rejoicing. 
\b
\m 
\v 18 Day after day, from the first day to the last, \zwd Ezra|Q191787\zwd* read from the Book of the Law of God. The Israelites kept the feast for seven days, and on the eighth day they held an assembly, according to the ordinance. 
\c 9
\s1 The People Confess Their Sins
\m 
\v 1 On the twenty-fourth day of the same month, the Israelites gathered together, fasting and wearing sackcloth, with dust on their heads. 
\v 2 Those of Israelite descent separated themselves from all the foreigners, and they stood and confessed their sins and the iniquities of their fathers. 
\b
\m 
\v 3 While they stood in their places, they read from the Book of the Law of the LORD their God for a quarter of the day, and they spent another quarter of the day in confession and worship of the LORD their God. 
\b
\m 
\v 4 And the Levites—\zwd Jeshua|\zwd*, \zwd Bani|Q139694083\zwd*, \zwd Kadmiel|\zwd*, \zwd Shebaniah|Q140185477\zwd*, \zwd Bunni|\zwd*, \zwd Sherebiah|\zwd*, \zwd Bani|\zwd*, and \zwd Chenani|Q125503923\zwd*—stood on the raised platform and cried out in a loud voice to the LORD their God. 
\v 5 Then the Levites—\zwd Jeshua|\zwd*, \zwd Kadmiel|\zwd*, \zwd Bani|Q139694083\zwd*, \zwd Hashabneiah|\zwd*, \zwd Sherebiah|\zwd*, \zwd Hodiah|\zwd*, \zwd Shebaniah|Q140185477\zwd*, and \zwd Pethahiah|\zwd*—said, “Stand up and bless the LORD your God from everlasting to everlasting: 
\b
\q1 Blessed be Your glorious name, 
\q2 and may it be exalted 
\q2 above all blessing and praise. 
\q1 
\v 6 You alone are the LORD. 
\q2 You created the heavens, 
\q1 the highest heavens with all their host, 
\q2 the earth and all that is on it, 
\q2 the seas and all that is in them. 
\q1 You give life to all things, 
\q2 and the host of heaven worships You. 
\b
\q1 
\v 7 You are the LORD, 
\q2 the God who chose \zwd Abram|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*, 
\q1 who brought him out of Ur of the Chaldeans 
\q2 and gave him the name \zwd Abraham|id="Q9181" t="2"\zwd*. 
\q1 
\v 8 You found his heart faithful before You, 
\q2 and made a covenant with him 
\q1 to give the land of the Canaanites and Hittites, 
\q2 of the Amorites and Perizzites, 
\q2 of the Jebusites and Girgashites—
\q1 to give it to his descendants. 
\q2 You have kept Your promise, 
\q2 because You are righteous. 
\b
\q1 
\v 9 You saw the affliction of our fathers in Egypt; 
\q2 You heard their cry at the Red Sea.\f + \fr 9:9 \ft Or the Sea of Reeds\f* 
\q1 
\v 10 You performed signs and wonders against \zwd Pharaoh|\zwd*, 
\q2 all his officials, and all the people of his land, 
\q1 for You knew they had acted with arrogance 
\q2 against our fathers. 
\q1 You made a name for Yourself 
\q2 that endures to this day. 
\q1 
\v 11 You divided the sea before them, 
\q2 and they crossed through it on dry ground. 
\q1 You hurled their pursuers into the depths 
\q2 like a stone into raging waters. 
\b
\q1 
\v 12 You led them with a pillar of cloud by day 
\q2 and a pillar of fire by night, 
\q1 to light for them the way 
\q2 in which they should travel. 
\q1 
\v 13 You came down on Mount Sinai 
\q2 and spoke with them from heaven. 
\q1 You gave them just ordinances, true laws, 
\q2 and good statutes and commandments. 
\q1 
\v 14 You revealed to them Your holy Sabbath 
\q2 and gave them commandments and statutes and laws 
\q2 through Your servant \zwd Moses|id="Q9077" t="2"\zwd*. 
\q1 
\v 15 In their hunger You gave them bread from heaven; 
\q2 in their thirst You brought them water from the rock. 
\q1 You told them to go in and possess the land 
\q2 that You had sworn to give them. 
\b
\q1 
\v 16 But they and our fathers became arrogant and stiff-necked 
\q2 and did not obey Your commandments. 
\q1 
\v 17 They refused to listen and failed to remember 
\q2 the wonders You performed among them. 
\q1 They stiffened their necks and appointed a leader 
\q2 to return them to their bondage in Egypt.\f + \fr 9:17 \ft LXX and a few Hebrew manuscripts; MT to return them to their bondage in their rebellion\f* 
\q1 But You are a forgiving God, 
\q2 gracious and compassionate, 
\q1 slow to anger and rich in loving devotion, 
\q2 and You did not forsake them. 
\b
\q1 
\v 18 Even when they cast for themselves 
\q2 an image of a calf and said, 
\q1 ‘This is your God who brought you up out of Egypt,’ 
\q2 and when they committed terrible blasphemies, 
\q1 
\v 19 You in Your great compassion 
\q2 did not forsake them in the wilderness. 
\q1 By day the pillar of cloud never turned away 
\q2 from guiding them on their path; 
\q1 and by the night the pillar of fire 
\q2 illuminated the way they should go. 
\q1 
\v 20 You gave Your good Spirit to instruct them. 
\q2 You did not withhold Your manna from their mouths, 
\q2 and You gave them water for their thirst. 
\q1 
\v 21 For forty years You sustained them in the wilderness, 
\q2 so that they lacked nothing. 
\q1 Their clothes did not wear out 
\q2 and their feet did not swell. 
\b
\q1 
\v 22 You gave them kingdoms and peoples 
\q2 and allotted to them every corner of the land. 
\q1 So they took the land of \zwd Sihon \f + \fr 9:22 \ft One Hebrew manuscript and LXX; most Hebrew manuscripts Sihon, the country of the\f* king of Heshbon|id="Q887987" t="2"\zwd* 
\q2 and of \zwd Og king of Bashan|id="Q878675" t="2"\zwd*. 
\q1 
\v 23 You multiplied their descendants 
\q2 like the stars of heaven 
\q1 and brought them to the land 
\q2 You had told their fathers to enter and possess. 
\q1 
\v 24 So their descendants went in and possessed the land; 
\q2 You subdued before them the Canaanites dwelling in the land. 
\q1 You delivered into their hands the kings and peoples of the land, 
\q2 to do with them as they wished. 
\q1 
\v 25 They captured fortified cities and fertile land 
\q2 and took houses full of all goods, 
\q1 wells already dug, 
\q2 vineyards, olive groves, and fruit trees in abundance. 
\q1 So they ate and were filled; 
\q2 they grew fat and delighted in Your great goodness. 
\b
\q1 
\v 26 But they were disobedient and rebelled against You; 
\q2 they flung Your law behind their backs. 
\q1 They killed Your prophets, 
\q2 who had admonished them to return to You. 
\q2 They committed terrible blasphemies. 
\q1 
\v 27 So You delivered them into the hands 
\q2 of enemies who oppressed them, 
\q2 and in their time of distress they cried out to You. 
\q1 From heaven You heard them, 
\q2 and in Your great compassion You gave them deliverers 
\q2 who saved them from the hands of their enemies. 
\q1 
\v 28 But as soon as they had rest, 
\q2 they again did evil in Your sight. 
\q1 So You abandoned them to the hands of their enemies, 
\q2 who had dominion over them. 
\q1 When they cried out to You again, 
\q2 You heard from heaven, 
\q1 and You delivered them many times 
\q2 in Your compassion. 
\b
\q1 
\v 29 You admonished them to turn back to Your law, 
\q2 but they were arrogant and disobeyed Your commandments. 
\q1 They sinned against Your ordinances, 
\q2 by which a man will live if he practices them. 
\q1 They turned a stubborn shoulder; 
\q2 they stiffened their necks and would not obey. 
\q1 
\v 30 You were patient with them for many years, 
\q2 and Your Spirit admonished them through Your prophets. 
\q1 Yet they would not listen, 
\q2 so You gave them into the hands of the neighboring peoples.\f + \fr 9:30 \ft Literally into the hands of the peoples of the lands\f* 
\q1 
\v 31 But in Your great compassion, 
\q2 You did not put an end to them; 
\q1 nor did You forsake them, 
\q2 for You are a gracious and compassionate God. 
\b
\q1 
\v 32 So now, our God, the great and mighty and awesome God 
\q2 who keeps His gracious covenant, 
\q1 do not view lightly all the hardship 
\q2 that has come upon us, 
\q1 and upon our kings and leaders, 
\q2 our priests and prophets, 
\q1 our ancestors and all Your people, 
\q2 from the days of the kings of Assyria until today. 
\q1 
\v 33 You are just in all that has befallen us, 
\q2 because You have acted faithfully, 
\q2 while we have acted wickedly. 
\q1 
\v 34 Our kings and leaders and priests and fathers 
\q2 did not obey Your law 
\q1 or listen to Your commandments 
\q2 and warnings that You gave them. 
\q1 
\v 35 For even while they were in their kingdom, 
\q2 with the abundant goodness 
\q2 that You had given them, 
\q1 and in the spacious and fertile land 
\q2 that You had set before them, 
\q1 they would not serve You 
\q2 or turn from their wicked ways. 
\b
\q1 
\v 36 So here we are today as slaves 
\q2 in the land You gave our fathers to enjoy its fruit and goodness—
\q2 here we are as slaves! 
\q1 
\v 37 Its abundant harvest goes to the kings 
\q2 You have set over us because of our sins. 
\q1 And they rule over our bodies and our livestock as they please. 
\q2 We are in great distress. 
\b
\m 
\v 38 In view of all this, we make a binding agreement, putting it in writing and sealing it with the names of \f + \fr 9:38 \ft Hebrew does not include the names of.\f* our leaders, Levites, and priests.” 
\c 10
\s1 Signers of the Covenant
\m 
\v 1 Now these were the ones who sealed the document: 
\b
\li1 \zwd Nehemiah the governor|Q1025598\zwd*, son of \zwd Hacaliah|id="Q5637034" t="2"\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 and also \zwd Zedekiah|Q138720623\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 2 \zwd Seraiah|\zwd*, \zwd Azariah|\zwd*, \zwd Jeremiah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
\v 3 \zwd Pashhur|\zwd*, \zwd Amariah|Q138721112\zwd*, \zwd Malchijah|\zwd*, 
\b
\li1 
